# Getting started

<p class="lede">
From nothing to a plugin running on an isolated dev daemon, reloading on every save, with a screenshot to prove it.
About five minutes, most of it <code>npm install</code>.
</p>

You need Node 22.18 or newer and the Paseo desktop app (it ships the Paseo CLI and the compiler `ppd check` uses).
The output below is real; home directories are shortened to `~` and temp directories to `$TMPDIR`.

<h2 id="create">1. Create a plugin</h2>

```sh
npx --package paseo-plugin-devkit ppd create ~/code/my-plugin
cd ~/code/my-plugin && npm install
```

```text
created my-plugin (panel template) in ~/code/my-plugin
  base: paseo plugin init
  .gitignore
  OVERVIEW.md
  README.md
  client/hello-panel.tsx
  index.client.tsx
  index.server.ts
  package.json
  paseo-plugin.json
  server/greet.ts
  shared/rpc.ts
  test/greet.test.ts
  test/host-contract.test.ts
  tsconfig.json
  vitest.config.ts

next:
  cd ~/code/my-plugin && npm install
  npm run check && npm test
  npx ppd daemon up && npx ppd dev
```

The base comes from `paseo plugin init`, so the SDK version matches your Paseo. `ppd create` adds what init leaves
out: tsconfig `node` types, an `@types/node` ~22 pin, a `.gitignore`, vitest with a host-contract suite, the npm
scripts `check`, `test`, `dev` and `typecheck`, and a README. The panel template is a workspace panel called "Hello",
an "Open Hello panel" Command Center item and one RPC, `hello.greet`.

`--template agent-hook` creates a plugin that reacts to agent turns instead: see the
[timeline renderer recipe](/docs/recipes/timeline-renderer).

<h2 id="check">2. Check it against the host</h2>

```sh
npm run check && npm test
```

```text
PASS  manifest         my-plugin, requires paseo >=0.12.0
PASS  imports          client, server and shared boundaries hold
PASS  dependencies     no runtime packages
PASS  paseo-compile    compilePlugin of the Paseo 0.12.0 app (/Applications/Paseo.app) accepted the plugin
PASS  client-requires  client requires only host modules (@getpaseo/plugin, @getpaseo/plugin/client, react, react-native, react/jsx-runtime, zod)
PASS  server-requires  server requires only host modules and Node built-ins
PASS  server-eval      Paseo's evaluator ran the contribution in 0 ms; RPCs: hello.greet
PASS  client-eval      contributions: addWorkspacePanel, addCommandCenterItem
PASS  hermes-class     no class syntax in the client bundle
PASS  hermes-parse     hermesc compiles the client bundle
PASS  hermes-vm        Hermes VM evaluated the bundle and ran the contribution (addWorkspacePanel, addCommandCenterItem)
PASS  lucide-icons     2 icon name(s) valid against 6354 Lucide icons of the Paseo 0.12.0 app (/Applications/Paseo.app)
PASS  bundle-size      client 5 KiB (2 KiB gzip), budget 2.0 MiB; server 2 KiB (1 KiB gzip), eval 0 ms, budget 32.0 MiB

13 passed, 0 warnings, 0 failed, 0 skipped
```

`npm test` runs the unit tests plus `test/host-contract.test.ts`, which turns every check into a vitest test. What
each line means, and what a failure looks like, is on [Host-contract checks](/docs/check).

<h2 id="daemon">3. Start a dev daemon</h2>

```sh
npx ppd daemon up --workspace .
```

```text
dev daemon "default" up
  url:   http://127.0.0.1:7800
  home:  $TMPDIR/ppd-default-2kCaQs
  pid:   84941
  paseo: 0.12.0
workspace created for ~/code/my-plugin
```

A detached Paseo daemon in a fresh home under `$TMPDIR`, on the first free port from 7800 to 7899, with plugins and
the web UI on and relay, MCP and speech off. Your own Paseo keeps running on port 6767, untouched. `--name` runs
several dev daemons side by side; `install`, `dev`, `paseo`, `web` and `android` take the same `--name`.

<h2 id="dev">4. Run the dev loop</h2>

```sh
npx ppd dev
```

```text
--- installing my-plugin into http://127.0.0.1:7800
my-plugin: running (installed) in 1865 ms
client: passes the app's registration rules
  log 2026-10-18T08:25:34.284Z  stdout    [paseo] Loading plugin
  log 2026-10-18T08:25:34.550Z  stdout    [paseo] Plugin ready
watching . (Ctrl+C to stop)
```

Edit `server/greet.ts` (here `Hello,` became `Hi,`) and the plugin reloads, the client is re-checked, and new plugin
log lines stream in:

```text
--- reloading: server/greet.ts
my-plugin: running (reloaded) in 1921 ms
client: passes the app's registration rules
  log 2026-10-18T08:25:43.954Z  stdout    [paseo] Stopping plugin
  log 2026-10-18T08:25:43.966Z  stdout    [paseo] Plugin stopped
  log 2026-10-18T08:25:43.966Z  stdout    [paseo] Loading plugin
  log 2026-10-18T08:25:44.247Z  stdout    [paseo] Plugin ready
```

`ppd install` does one install or reload without watching. Both report `client-rejected` when the app would drop
your client: see [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting#client-rejected).

<h2 id="web">5. Look at it</h2>

In another terminal:

```sh
npx ppd web --command "Open Hello panel" --click Greet --shot out/hello.png
```

```text
opened workspace my-plugin (wks_61e6e477b7e141dd)
screenshot: ~/code/my-plugin/out/hello.png
console errors: none
plugin evaluation errors: none
```

<Shot
  src="/shots/dev-loop-panel.png"
  alt="The Hello panel in the Paseo web UI after clicking Greet: 'Hi, Paseo! (from the plugin server on darwin) Call #1.'"
  width={1400}
  height={900}
  caption="The screenshot ppd web took: the panel, served by the dev daemon, after its RPC round trip with the edited greeting."
/>

`ppd web` drives Chrome through Playwright: it opens the workspace, runs a Command Center item, clicks elements by
their accessible name, and prints console errors and any plugin client the app failed to evaluate (that one exits 1).
`--viewport 390x844` shows the compact (phone) layout and `--headed` shows the browser, which stays open when there
is no `--shot`. On a real phone:
[Testing on a phone](/docs/android).

<h2 id="stop">6. Stop</h2>

```sh
npx ppd daemon down
```

```text
stopped dev daemon "default" (pid 84941)
removed $TMPDIR/ppd-default-2kCaQs
```

<Callout kind="tip" title="Agents">

The repo ships a Claude Code skill, `skills/paseo-plugin-dev`, that teaches an agent this loop and the safety rules.
Copy it into `~/.claude/skills/` (all projects) or the plugin's `.claude/skills/` and ask, for example, "verify my
plugin on a dev daemon and screenshot the panel".

</Callout>

<h2 id="existing">Add ppd to an existing plugin</h2>

```sh
npm install -D paseo-plugin-devkit
npx ppd check
```

Then make the checks part of your tests:

```ts
// test/host-contract.test.ts: one vitest test per check
import { fileURLToPath } from "node:url";
import { describeHostContract } from "paseo-plugin-devkit/vitest";

describeHostContract(fileURLToPath(new URL("..", import.meta.url)));
```

and copy [`examples/ci.yml`](https://github.com/nkwib/paseo-plugin-devkit/blob/main/examples/ci.yml) into
`.github/workflows/`. It runs `ppd check` (as a labeled approximation, since Linux runners have no Paseo app) and
`ppd registry precheck` on every push, plus a weekly job that checks your plugin against Paseo main, so upstream
changes break your CI before they break your users.
